.TH RAMFS 4
.SH NAME
ramfs \- the teaching file server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ramfs
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ramfs
posts
.B /srv/ramfs
and serves a small fixed tree out of its own program segments:
.IP
.EX
hello   read-only text, in this program's rodata
note    a writable file, in this program's bss
.EE
.PP
A read of
.B hello
returns bytes from a page the loader mapped read-only.
A write to
.B note
lands in a page the loader mapped writable, up to 300 bytes;
a write past that answers
.BR ENOSPC .
Neither file can be created, removed or renamed, and no other file can be
made: the tree
.I ramfs
serves never changes shape.
It takes no arguments, and there is no flag to change the name it posts.
.PP
A client sees the tree by mounting
.B /srv/ramfs
(see
.IR bind (1)).
.I Ramfs
is the smallest complete file server in the tree: two files, one read-only
and one writable, in the same handler shape a kernel device uses.
.PP
A
.B Tremove
on either file is answered and then ends the server.
The pipe's hang-up -- the last mount and the posted name both gone --
also ends it.
Either is a clean stop.
A frame that does not parse as a 9P message is a fault.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /srv/ramfs
the posted service
.TP
.B /hello
read-only, this program's own text about itself
.TP
.B /note
writable, 300 bytes
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/ramfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR serve (2),
.IR fid (2),
.IR vectra9 (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Ramfs
exits with a number rather than the empty-string convention other
programs use.
A
.B Tremove
ends it with status 0.
The pipe's hang-up ends it with 104.
A torn frame ends it with 114, and failing to post
.B /srv/ramfs
ends it with 113.
